striction": false}, "DOI": "10.1109/wi.2005.64", "type": "proceedings-article", "created": {"date-parts": [[2005, 10, 18]], "date-time": "2005-10-18T13:56:04Z", "timestamp": 1129643764000}, "page": "434-437", "source": "Crossref", "is-referenced-by-count": 6, "title": ["From Robots to Web-Agents: Building Cognitive Software Agents for Web-Information Retrieval by Taking Inspiration from Experience in Robotics"], "prefix": "10.1109", "author": [{"given": "A.", "family": "Revel", "sequence": "first", "affiliation": [], "role": [{"role": "author", "vocabulary": "crossref"}]}], "member": "263", "reference": [{"key": "15", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/ICMAS.1998.699290"}, {"key": "13", "first-page": "129", "article-title": "Planification versus sensory-motor conditioning: What are the issues?", "author": "revel", "year": "1998", "journal-title": "SAB'98"}, {"key": "14", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1177/105971239700500203"}, {"key": "11", "first-page": "120", "article-title": "How to build an imitator?", "author": "nadel", "year": "2003", "journal-title": "Second International Symposium on Imitation in Animals and Artifacts"}, {"key": "12", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/WI.2003.1241245"}, {"key": "3", "article-title": "Merging visual place recognition and path integration for cognitive map learning", "author": "babeau", "year": "2000", "journal-title": "The Sixth Int Conf on Simulation for Adaptive Behavior SAB'2000"}, {"key": "2", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1007/978-3-7908-1775-1_11"}, {"key": "1", "first-page": "104", "article-title": "Mobile software agents for control in telecommunications networks", "volume": "12", "author": "appleby", "year": "1994", "journal-title": "British Telecom Technology J"}, {"key": "10", "article-title": "From animals to animals", "author": "meyer", "year": "1991", "journal-title": "First International Conference on Simulation of Adaptive Behavior"}, {"key": "7", "article-title": "What can robots take for free? learning to build visual categories from sensori-motor associations", "author": "joulain", "year": "1996", "journal-title": "SAB'96"}, {"key": "6", "first-page": "123", "article-title": "Swarm-made architectures", "author": "deneubourg", "year": "1992", "journal-title": "Proceedings of the first european conference on artificial intelligence"}, {"key": "5", "first-page": "356", "article-title": "The dynamics of collective sorting: Robot-like ants and ant-like robots", "author": "deneubourg", "year": "1990", "journal-title": "From Animals to Animals Proc First Int Conference on Simulation of Adaptive Behavior"}, {"key": "4", "doi-asserted-by": "crossref", "first-page": "39", "DOI": "10.1038/35017500", "article-title": "The social insect paradigm for optimization and control", "volume": "406", "author": "bonabeau", "year": "2000", "journal-title": "Nature"}, {"key": "9", "doi-asserted-by": "publisher", "DOI": "10.1109/3477.484436"}, {"key": "8", "article-title": "Impact of imitation on the dynamics of animat populations in a spatial cognition task", "author": "laroque", "year": "2004", "journal-title": "Intelligent Autonomous Systems 7"}], "event": {"name": "The 2005 IEEE/WIC/ACM International Conference on Web Intelligence (WI'05)", "location": "Compiegne, France"}, "container-title": ["The 2005 IEEE/WIC/ACM International Conference on Web Intelligence (WI'05)"], "link": [{"URL": "http://xplorestaging.ieee.org/ielx5/10179/32502/01517887.pdf?arnumber=1517887", "content-type": "unspecified", "content-version": "vor", "intended-application": "similarity-checking"}], "deposited": {"date-parts": [[2017, 6, 17]], "date-time": "2017-06-17T00:12:48Z", "timestamp": 1497658368000}, "score": 22.737873, "resource": {"primary": {"URL": "http://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/1517887/"}}, "issued": {"date-parts": [[null]]}, "references-count": 15, "URL": "https://doi.org/10.1109/wi.2005.64"}, {"indexed": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 5]], "date-time": "2026-03-05T00:09:18Z", "timestamp": 1772669358946, "version": "3.50.1"}, "posted": {"date-parts": [[2025, 5, 8]]}, "group-title": "JMIR Human Factors", "reference-count": 41, "publisher": "JMIR Publications Inc.", "content-domain": {"domain": [], "crossmark-restriction": false}, "abstract": "<sec>\n                  <title>BACKGROUND</title>\n                  <p>Lived experience stories are often used on formal help sites as a support resource for individuals who self-harm. While self-harm\u2013related internet use provides an alternative for individuals who are not yet ready or are unwilling or unable to access support offline, it has also been shown to unintentionally reinforce self-harm behavior. There are several components that might influence whether a lived experience story is perceived as helpful, unhelpful, or potentially harmful, and the evidence supporting that these encourage help-seeking in the reader is limited.</p>\n                </sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>OBJECTIVE</title>\n                  <p>This study is part of a mixed methods project that aimed to investigate how variations in help-seeking messages contained within online lived experience stories are interpreted by and psychologically impact those with a history of self-harm.</p>\n                </sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>METHODS</title>\n                  <p>Individuals with a recent history of self-harm were recruited via newsletters, social media, and websites run by the university and mental health charities to take part in an online experiment. During the experiment, participants were randomized to read stories that either mentioned (1) self-help strategies, (2) seeking help from informal and formal sources, or (3) did not mention help-seeking. Help-seeking intentions, mood, entrapment, and expectations of future self-harm were measured, and participants provided feedback on the stories.</p>\n                </sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>RESULTS</title>\n                  <p>There was limited evidence for an effect of story type on help-seeking intentions (&lt;i&gt;F&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2, 230&lt;/sub&gt;=4.2; &lt;i&gt;P&lt;/i&gt;=.02; \u03b7&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;=0.25), and clearer evidence for an effect of story type on negative affect (&lt;i&gt;F&lt;/i&gt;&lt;sub&gt;2, 230&lt;/sub&gt;=4.02; &lt;i&gt;P&lt;/i&gt;=.02; \u03b7&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;=0.10; adjustment for age, gender, and help-seeking history included). Participants in the \u201cself-help\u201d condition (n=83) reported lower negative affect after reading the stories compared to participants in the \u201cno help\u201d condition (n=80; mean difference=\u20133.97, 95% CI \u20137.72 to \u20130.22; &lt;i&gt;P&lt;/i&gt;=.04) and the \u201cinformal/formal\u201d help condition (n=75; mean difference=\u20133.70, 95% CI \u20137.55 to 0.14; &lt;i&gt;P&lt;/i&gt;=.06). A key criticism of the stories was that they were unrelatable, but this sentiment was less prevalent among those in the \u201cno help\u201d condition. Key positives were that the stories included a realistic but hopeful outlook of recovery (less prevalent in the \u201cinformal/formal help\u201d condition) and were supportive (less prevalent in the \u201cno help\u201d condition).</p>\n                </sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CONCLUSIONS</title>\n                  <p>While the inclusion of self-help strategies in a lived experience story reduced its impact on negative affect, the inclusion of self-help strategies or seeking help from others did not encourage help-seeking intentions. Making stories relatable, authentic, and providing multiple strategies for support might be key to encouraging help-seeking, but more research is needed.</p>\n                </sec>", "DOI": "10.2196/preprints.71280", "type": "posted-content", "created": {"date-parts": [[2026, 3, 4]], "date-time": "2026-03-04T23:50:09Z", "timestamp":